## PART 1: PEOPLE MANAGEMENT

### Q1. Tell me about your experience managing engineering teams, and how your leadership style has evolved.

**A:** Over 17+ years I've moved from individual contributor to leading teams of 8–10 engineers, most recently at JPMorgan Chase where I lead scrum teams delivering AI-powered fraud detection platforms, and previously at OpenText where I managed a team of 8 end-to-end on an enterprise SaaS platform, and at IBM where I led a cross-functional team of 10 across Engineering and Product for our data governance platform.

My style has shifted from being the "answer person" early in my career to being an enabler — setting clear technical direction and guardrails, then giving engineers ownership of how they get there. At IBM, for example, I owned the roadmap for our Catalog service but let individual engineers propose and defend their own design approaches in review, which is also where I did most of my coaching. I try to run 1:1s that are forward-looking (career growth, blockers) rather than status-only, since status belongs in stand-ups.

### Q2. Describe a time you coached or developed an engineer who was struggling.

**A:** *(Situation)* On the IBM data governance platform, I had an engineer who was technically strong but whose PRs were routinely rejected in review because of scope creep and inconsistent design patterns, which was slowing the team down and causing frustration on both sides.

*(Task)* I needed to get this person delivering independently at the team's pace without losing their strong technical instincts.

*(Action)* Rather than just calling out the PR issues, I paired with them on a design doc before code was written, so we agreed on scope and pattern up front — this shifted feedback earlier in the cycle where it's cheaper to act on. I also gave them ownership of a self-contained piece of the Watson Search recommender service so they could build confidence with fast, visible wins, and we did a regular 1:1 check-in focused specifically on this growth area, not general status.

*(Result)* Within about two quarters, their PRs were passing review on the first pass most of the time, and they went on to independently own a meaningful chunk of the GraphQL API backend. It reinforced for me that most "review friction" problems are actually upstream design-alignment problems.

### Q3. How do you set expectations and manage performance for your team?

**A:** I set expectations at two levels. At the team level, sprint goals and Definition of Done are explicit and visible (we used JIRA boards and sprint burn-downs at IBM and OpenText so everyone — including stakeholders — could see progress without me having to relay it). At the individual level, I set quarterly goals tied to both delivery (what they're shipping) and growth (a skill or scope they're stretching into), and I check in on both in 1:1s rather than waiting for a formal review cycle to surface issues.

When performance is off-track, I try to have that conversation early and specifically — pointing to concrete examples, agreeing on what "better" looks like, and giving a defined timeframe to show improvement — rather than letting it simmer until a review. I'd rather have an uncomfortable conversation in week 2 than a surprised one in month 6.

### Q4. Tell me about a time you had to build or grow a team, including identifying resourcing needs.

**A:** *(Situation)* At OpenText, when I took on the AI SaaS platform, I inherited a partial team and had to figure out where the gaps were relative to the roadmap we'd committed to with Product and Business stakeholders.

*(Task)* I needed to right-size the team — both headcount and skill mix — to hit a roadmap that spanned CI/CD, a Spring Boot/Java 17 data pipeline, and a new GenAI retrieval capability we hadn't built before.

*(Action)* I broke the roadmap into workstreams and mapped current team skills against them, which made it clear we were strong on core backend/Java but light on anything GenAI/vector-retrieval related. I flagged that gap early to my leadership with a clear ask, and in the meantime I upskilled a couple of engineers on the existing team into that space myself, since I'd been doing hands-on GenAI work.

*(Result)* We shipped the vector-based retrieval system and the AI-assisted developer productivity tooling on the original roadmap timeline, and two engineers came out of it with a genuinely new skill set, which made the team more resilient going forward.

> **Note:** The JD calls out "identifying resource needs, interviewing candidates, and onboarding new engineers" specifically. If you've personally run interview loops or made hiring decisions, be ready with a concrete example — the resume as written emphasizes team leadership and upskilling more than end-to-end recruiting, so it's worth clarifying your actual hands-on hiring experience before this comes up live.

### Q5. How do you foster a culture of innovation on your team?

**A:** I try to protect explicit space for it rather than hoping it happens organically. At JPMorgan, a lot of what became the on-device AI inference work (ExecuTorch for real-time fraud scoring) started as something closer to a side investigation into whether we could get latency and privacy wins by moving inference to the device — it wasn't in the original roadmap. I gave that room to prove itself with a small time-boxed spike before committing broader team time to it.

More broadly, I think innovation culture is really a byproduct of psychological safety — engineers propose new approaches when they're not worried about being wrong in front of the team. I try to model that by being explicit when my own initial approach on something didn't pan out.

### Q6. Tell me about a conflict within your team or with another team, and how you resolved it.

**A:** *(Situation)* At IBM, my engineering team and a downstream consumer team disagreed on the deduplication strategy for the Catalog service — my team wanted an approach optimized for ingestion throughput, theirs wanted one optimized for their query patterns, and each thought the other's approach would break their side.

*(Task)* I needed a resolution that didn't just default to "my team wins" but was based on actual data and trade-offs, since this was going to be a long-lived architectural decision.

*(Action)* I asked both teams to bring numbers, not opinions, to a joint design session — actual throughput and query-latency requirements. We ended up designing an under-deduplication strategy that met both teams' constraints, but the key unlock was reframing the conversation from "whose approach" to "what does the data say."

*(Result)* The strategy shipped, reduced resource and infrastructure costs as intended, and — maybe more importantly — that data-first pattern became how we resolved most cross-team architecture disagreements after that.

